::ALG2_U04_L04_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>2</sub>(8)? {
= 3#Correct. 2 raised to the third power equals 8.
~ 2#2 squared is 4, not 8.
~ 4#2 to the fourth power is 16.
~ 16#This is not the exponent needed.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>3</sub>(81)? {
~ 3#3 cubed is 27.
= 4#Correct. 3 to the fourth power equals 81.
~ 9#3 to the ninth power is much larger than 81.
~ 27#This is not the exponent needed.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>10</sub>(1000)? {
~ 2#10 squared equals 100.
~ 10#10 to the tenth power is too large.
= 3#Correct. 10 cubed equals 1000.
~ 100#This is not the exponent needed.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which exponential form matches log<sub>4</sub>(64)=3? {
~ 64<sup>3</sup> = 4#This reverses input and base.
~ 3<sup>4</sup> = 64#This uses the output as the base.
~ 4 + 3 = 64#Logarithms convert to exponent equations, not addition.
= 4<sup>3</sup> = 64#Correct. The base 4 raised to the logarithm output 3 equals 64.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which logarithmic form matches 5<sup>2</sup> = 25? {
= log<sub>5</sub>(25)=2#Correct. The base is 5, the input is 25, and the output is 2.
~ log<sub>25</sub>(5)=2#This reverses base and input.
~ log<sub>5</sub>(2)=25#This makes the exponent the input.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(25)=5#This uses the exponent as the base.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the domain of y=log<sub>2</sub>(x)? {
~ x &ge; 0#Zero is not allowed.
= x &gt; 0#Correct. Logarithmic inputs must be positive.
~ all real numbers#Negative and zero inputs are not in the basic log domain.
~ x &ne; 2#The base does not create this restriction.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the range of y=log<sub>2</sub>(x)? {
~ x &gt; 0#That describes the domain, not the range.
~ y &gt; 0#Logarithm outputs can be negative, zero, or positive.
= all real numbers#Correct. Logarithmic outputs can be any real number.
~ y &ne; 0#The output can be 0 when x = 1.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the vertical asymptote of y=log<sub>3</sub>(x)? {
~ y = 0#That is the horizontal asymptote of the exponential inverse.
~ x = 3#The base does not become the vertical asymptote.
~ y = 3#This does not describe the asymptote.
= x = 0#Correct. The basic logarithmic graph approaches the y-axis.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which point is on y=log<sub>2</sub>(x)? {
= (8, 3)#Correct. Because log<sub>2</sub>(8)=3.
~ (3, 8)#This reverses the coordinates.
~ (2, 8)#log<sub>2</sub>(2)=1, not 8.
~ (0, 2)#x=0 is not in the domain.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>4</sub>(1)? {
~ 1#4 to the first power is 4.
= 0#Correct. Any positive base to the zero power equals 1.
~ 4#4 to the fourth power is 256.
~ undefined#The logarithm of 1 is defined for valid positive bases not equal to 1.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>7</sub>(7)? {
~ 0#7 to the zero power equals 1.
~ 7#7 to the seventh power is too large.
= 1#Correct. 7 to the first power equals 7.
~ 49#This is not the needed exponent.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is true about y=log<sub>2</sub>(x) and y=2<sup>x</sup>? {
~ They are the same graph.#Inverse graphs reflect across y = x, so they are not identical.
~ Both have domain x &gt; 0.#The exponential domain is all real numbers.
~ Both have range y &gt; 0.#The logarithmic range is all real numbers.
= They are inverse functions.#Correct. They reverse inputs and outputs.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>5</sub>(125)? {
= 3#Correct. 5 cubed equals 125.
~ 2#5 squared equals 25.
~ 4#5 to the fourth is 625.
~ 25#This is not the exponent needed.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which form matches log<sub>b</sub>(a)=c? {
~ a<sup>c</sup> = b#This reverses the base and input.
= b<sup>c</sup> = a#Correct. The base raised to the log output equals the input.
~ b + c = a#The relationship is exponential, not additive.
~ c<sup>b</sup> = a#This uses the output as the base.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>2</sub>(1/8)? {
~ 3#2 cubed equals 8, not 1/8.
~ -8#This is not the exponent needed.
= -3#Correct. 2 to the negative third power equals 1/8.
~ 1/3#2 to the one-third power is not 1/8.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>9</sub>(9)? {
~ 0#9 to the zero power equals 1.
~ 2#9 squared equals 81.
~ 18#This is not the exponent needed.
= 1#Correct. 9 to the first power equals 9.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which graph feature belongs to y=log<sub>10</sub>(x)? {
= Vertical asymptote x = 0#Correct. Basic logarithmic functions approach x = 0 from the right.
~ Horizontal asymptote y = 0#That belongs to the exponential inverse.
~ Vertex at (0,0)#Logarithmic graphs do not have a vertex.
~ Domain all real numbers#The domain is positive x-values.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>6</sub>(36)? {
~ 3#6 cubed equals 216.
= 2#Correct. 6 squared equals 36.
~ 6#6 to the sixth power is much larger.
~ 30#This subtracts instead of using exponents.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which logarithm equals 4? {
~ log<sub>4</sub>(2)#4 to the one-half power equals 2, not 4.
~ log<sub>16</sub>(2)#16 to the one-fourth power equals 2.
= log<sub>2</sub>(16)#Correct. 2 to the fourth power equals 16.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(8)#This equals 3.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What does log<sub>3</sub>(x)=5 mean? {
~ x<sup>5</sup> = 3#This reverses the input and base.
~ 3x = 5#This treats the relationship as multiplication.
~ x + 3 = 5#This treats the relationship as addition.
= 3<sup>5</sup> = x#Correct. The log output is the exponent on base 3.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the inverse of y=10<sup>x</sup>? {
= y=log<sub>10</sub>(x)#Correct. A logarithm with base 10 reverses the exponential function.
~ y=x<sup>10</sup>#This is a power function, not the inverse exponential.
~ y=10x#This is linear.
~ y=x/10#This is linear division.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which input is not allowed in y=log<sub>4</sub>(x)? {
~ x = 1#log<sub>4</sub>(1)=0 is defined.
= x = 0#Correct. Logarithmic inputs must be positive, and zero is not allowed.
~ x = 4#log<sub>4</sub>(4)=1 is defined.
~ x = 16#log<sub>4</sub>(16)=2 is defined.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which point on y=2<sup>x</sup> corresponds to (8,3) on y=log<sub>2</sub>(x)? {
~ (8, 3)#That is the logarithmic point, not the corresponding exponential point.
~ (2, 3)#This does not switch the coordinates correctly.
= (3, 8)#Correct. Inverse functions switch input and output.
~ (0, 8)#This is not the inverse point.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>4</sub>(16)? {
~ 4#4 to the fourth is 256.
~ 8#4 to the eighth is too large.
~ 12#This subtracts instead of using exponent reasoning.
= 2#Correct. 4 squared equals 16.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>2</sub>(32)? {
= 5#Correct. 2 to the fifth power equals 32.
~ 4#2 to the fourth is 16.
~ 6#2 to the sixth is 64.
~ 16#This is not the exponent needed.
}

